    "rawContent": "---\nname: sap\ndescription: Sapling (sl) specific workflows and commands for efficient VCS operations\ncategory: workflow\ntriggers:\n  - /sap\naliases:\n  - /sap\n\nsuggest:\n  - /git-sapling\n  - /sp\n  - /scratchpad\n---\n\n# SAP - Sapling VCS Workflows\n\nSapling (sl) specific workflows for efficient version control operations.\n\n## Purpose\n\nSapling (sl) specific workflows and commands for efficient VCS operations optimized for large monorepos and distributed development.\n\n## Project Context\n\n### Constitution/Constraints\n- Sapling-specific (not git) for projects with .sl directory\n- Efficient for large monorepos\n- Solo-developer optimized workflows\n\n### Technical Context\n- Main documentation: `P:/__csf/src/csf/cli/nip/sap.md`\n- VCS: Sapling (sl), not git\n- Optimized for: large monorepos, distributed development, intelligent rebasing, stack-based commits\n\n### Architecture Alignment\n- Integrates with `/git-sapling` for VCS guidance\n- Works alongside `/sp` for scratchpad worktrees\n- Suggests `/scratchpad` for isolated development\n\n## Your Workflow\n\n1. **Status Check**: `sl status` or `/sap status`\n2. **Atomic Operations**: Commits, rebase workflows, stack management\n3. **Efficient Operations**: Leverage Sapling's intelligent rebasing and stack management\n\n### Common Operations\n- Status checking\n- Atomic commits\n- Rebase workflows\n- Stack management\n\n## Validation Rules\n\n### VCS Selection Rule\n- **P:\\ root**: Use `git` only (never `sl`)\n- **Projects with .sl**: Use `sl` (Sapling)\n\n### Prohibited Actions\n- Do NOT use `sl` commands at P:\\ root\n- Do NOT use `git` commands in projects with .sl directory\n- Do NOT mix VCS operations within same project\n\n## Quick Start\n\n```bash\n/sap status\n/sap commit \"message\"\n```\n\n## Main Documentation\n\n`P:/__csf/src/csf/cli/nip/sap.md`\n\n## Purpose\n\nSapling provides efficient version control operations with workflows optimized for:\n- Large monorepos\n- Distributed development\n- Intelligent rebasing\n- Stack-based commits\n\n## Common Operations\n\n- Status checking\n- Atomic commits\n- Rebase workflows\n- Stack management\n"
